{
"id": "u04129",
"e": "      <p class=\"hint\">Administration and domestic service account for 59% of the inscriptions that name an occupation. They also have the highest share of the imperial household (administration: 28%, domestic service: 24%). Not a single imperial slave or freedman appears among the traders. Among craftsmen and traders, where a status is stated it is mostly “freed” (crafts: 72 of 102, trade: 20 of 25).</p>"
},
{
"id": "u04133",
"e": "      <p class=\"hint\">The time curve is built the same way as on the “Inscriptions” card (25-year bands, 2,139 dated inscriptions). 59% of the inscriptions naming an occupation fall in the 1st century AD, the age of the great columbaria. Measured against all dated inscriptions they are also most frequent then: 9.0% in the first half of the 1st century AD, 2.5% in the 3rd century.</p>"
},
